cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  LTLC — Leakage-Controlled Evaluation of Long-Tail Correction for
  Hyperspectral Image Classification
message: >-
  If you use this repository, please cite it. Please ALSO cite the original
  QUH dataset authors and the original HybridSN paper; neither is a
  contribution of this work.
type: software
# NOTE: no license field - repository licensing is pending confirmation.
#       See LICENSE_PENDING.md.
authors:
  - name: "CSE498R LTLC project contributors"
repository-code: ""
abstract: >-
  A leakage-controlled study of long-tail correction for hyperspectral image
  classification on the QUH benchmark using a fixed HybridSN backbone. A
  conventional pixel-random split yields approximately 99.99% cross-entropy
  validation accuracy because spatial patches around adjacent pixels overlap;
  under a spatially disjoint split with a guard band removing training pixels
  within Chebyshev distance 2r of any validation pixel, the same model family
  scores 74-88%. Under that protocol, standard training-prior correction gives
  a suggestive class-balanced gain on one scene and a marginal gain on the
  other, while a proposed rarity-adaptive term (LTLC) is not selected: a
  revised identifiable formulation chose a rarity coefficient of exactly zero
  on both scenes. A mechanism analysis shows training-frequency rarity
  correlates with cross-entropy class error at rho = +0.41 on one scene and
  -0.37 on the other, indicating that class frequency is not a consistent
  proxy for class difficulty. All differences are reported as suggestive
  rather than confirmatory; with three seeds an exact sign-flip test cannot
  produce a p-value below 0.25.
keywords:
  - hyperspectral image classification
  - long-tail learning
  - class imbalance
  - logit adjustment
  - spatially disjoint validation
  - data leakage
  - model calibration
  - negative result
  - HybridSN
  - QUH
